ships collectively characterize EBRT as dependent on specialized hardware for generating and aiming beams, rather than implanted sources or internal delivery methods. The data does not include information about vendors, manufacturers, or facility operators; it is limited to the functional role of the machine in therapy delivery. [Data: Entities (6116, 4772); Relationships (14022, 10548)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Targeting is organized around the cancer site and beam shaping (conformal radiation)\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes an explicit linkage between “cancer site” and EBRT: in EBRT, the machine aims radiation at the cancer site. This underscores that anatomical localization is a primary reference point for treatment targeting in this network. [Data: Entities (894, 634); Relationships (10543)].\\n\\nEBRT is also linked to conformal radiation, with the text stating that all types of EBRT are conformal (beam shaping to match the tumor). This indicates that shaping the radiation field to the target is a core planning concept in this community’s representation of EBRT. [Data: Entities (6132, 634); Relationships (14024)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Radiation modality selection depends on tumor depth: electrons for superficial tumors and photons for deeper tumors\",\"explanation\":\"EBRT is linked to “superficial tumor” and “deeper, more invasive tumor,” with electrons described as commonly used for superficial tumors and photons used for deeper, more invasive tumors. This reflects a practical technical decision rule in the dataset: radiation type selection is aligned with the depth of the target. [Data: Entities (648, 649, 634); Relationships (903, 904)].\\n\\nThe presence of both depth-related entities indicates the community captures treatment adaptation to anatomy and disease characteristics, but it does not provide quantitative thresholds, planning parameters, or outcome comparisons—only the qualitative mapping of electrons vs photons by depth. [Data: Entities (648, 6